"""
Interactive hierarchical block placer.

Adapted from graph_visualizer.py for real hierarchical placement data: a nodefile
describing blocks and ports, and an edge file listing connections between them.

    hier_place.py <design>.pseudo.nodefile <design>.pseudo.txt

Nodefile format (written by electron):
    DBU 4000
    DIEAREA 0 0 503.845 503.845
    ROWHEIGHT 0.2
    NODES 9518
    <node> <name> INST|PORT <width> <height> <status> [<x> <y>] TYPE=<type>

<x> <y> is the LOWER LEFT corner, as electron and DEF give it. It is read and written
that way so a placed nodefile goes straight back into the flow; the conversion to and
from the centre the physics works in happens only in the reader and the writer.

Edge file: one connection per line, two node indices referring to the <node> column.

Two things differ from the generic visualizer and drive the whole design:

  * World coordinates are microns, not pixels. The die box is drawn to scale and blocks
    keep their real footprint, so what you see is a placement rather than a diagram.

  * The edge file lists every connection separately, so a pair of blocks appears
    thousands of times over. Those are aggregated into one weighted spring per pair -
    treating them as thousands of independent springs would both crawl and let raw
    net count swamp the geometry.

  * Pins are placed, not simulated. A pin sits on a die edge and the only question is
    where along it, so each pin goes to the point on its edge nearest the blocks it
    connects to, and the pins of a layer are then packed in that order. Blocks feel
    their pins through one aggregated pull per block per edge. Giving every pin its own
    spring instead means thousands of springs acting on a handful of blocks: they
    outweigh the block-to-block connectivity, the blocks oscillate, and the pins chase
    them without ever settling.

Pins are spread over the routing layers of their edge (--layers, half serving the
horizontal edges and half the vertical), so with L layers per edge the pins on any one
layer are L apart and the usable pitch is L times the pin pitch.

Controls:
  Left-click drag a block to move it, mouse wheel to zoom, right-click drag to pan.
  FIXED nodes are drawn hatched and never move. 'Save' writes a placed nodefile.
"""

__author__ = "Rajeev Srivastava"
__copyright__ = "Copyright © 2025-2026 Tenstorrent AI ULC"
